  "prompt": "Use case: precise-object-edit / style-transfer. Asset type: a TWO-FLOOR GAME BACKGROUND ATLAS, editing the supplied image itself. The image has two rectangular rooms stacked vertically, upper floor 1 and lower floor 2. Keep this exact two-panel composition, their equal-width rectangular framing, panel split position, all floor planes, door openings, wall outlines, grid alignment, big furniture locations and footprints, central hologram table positions and overall dimensions. Preserve the original crisp detailed 2D pixel-art game background style and the exact fixed top-down/three-quarter orthographic camera. This is an environmental SET-DRESSING edit of these existing rooms, not a redesigned floor plan.\n\nPRIMARY CHANGE: this cyberpunk city's zombie apocalypse has happened. These two floors are now the fortified, improvised safehouse where the protagonist and friendly NPCs survive. Blend the scavenged, lived-in survival-shelter feeling of Fallout settlements with the existing cyberpunk design. Make the survival-apocalypse half of the theme immediately visible, but still a protected, functioning inhabited refuge. Retain ALL of the distinctive colorful neon signs and their original locations: pink noodle bowl, cyan light bars, medical cross, yellow wrench, colored vending displays, cyan holographic city maps, magenta and cyan door lights. Preserve strong readable saturated neon lighting and subtle floor reflections; do not wash the scene in a brown or gray filter.\n\nUPPER PANEL / FLOOR 1: lived-in communal living, ration kitchen and emergency clinic. Keep the upper-left neon diner/bar in its exact footprint, now scratched and repaired, crowded with salvaged ration tins, canned food, water containers, a small pot and mismatched supplies. Vending machines remain in place, visibly dented, with taped repairs and a few emergency ration boxes integrated into their existing footprint. The left lounge remains where it is, now patched blankets on the battered sofa, rolled sleeping bags and a survival duffel tucked into the existing lounge area. Keep the right upper medical chair/clinic in its exact footprint, now an improvised triage station with bandage boxes, disinfectant, taped cables, spare medical supplies and a survival checklist on its wall. The central cyan hologram table stays exactly where it is and the same size, with paper outbreak maps, radio and emergency notes attached to the existing table edges. Existing lower-right equipment bay becomes patched fortification/airlock repair equipment with scrap plates, sandbags packed against its perimeter inside its original footprint. A few bottles, wrappers, small flat paper scraps and worn boot trails on floor near furniture.\n\nLOWER PANEL / FLOOR 2: rough survivor armory, repair workshop, communications and scavenged supply storage. Existing lockers, weapons bench, central planning table, perimeter workbench and side desk MUST remain in place at exactly their existing scale. Add dented sheet-metal patches, taped wiring, stacked ammo tins and tool rolls on existing workbench surfaces, gas-mask/respirator equipment hanging on the wall, salvaged electronic parts, a hand-crank radio, improvised batteries, water cans and ration crates tucked INTO existing furniture footprints along the walls. Workbench is visibly greasy and in use; walls have rust streaks, repaired impacts and bolted scrap reinforcement. A little flat scrap, cloth, tape marks and cable runs at floor edges. The holographic planning table remains bright cyan and functional. This is the survivors' organized-enough defensive workshop, not a sterile futuristic laboratory.\n\nLAYOUT INVARIANTS: preserve every door opening at its exact location, including each wide central top door and the adjacent narrow green-signed door. Reinforce the wall/frame edges with salvaged steel but DO NOT place barricades in door openings or their approach paths. Keep the broad central walkable floors open and all passageways between existing big objects clear. Any new large/high object must fit wholly inside an EXISTING furniture footprint; elsewhere add only small flat walk-over debris, scuffs, footprints or stains. No new standalone piles or obstacles in open walking areas. Keep each original central table silhouette/position unchanged, keep walls and border strip aligned. Avoid much extra clutter immediately in front of service stations. Keep the same room sizes and all existing access routes.\n\nQUALITY: convincing carefully authored pixel art, colored pixel clusters, hard pixel edges matching the input, detailed but readable, same level of finish as original. Distress and signs of habitation should be deliberate and uneven, not a uniform dirt overlay. Neon remains recognizable through apocalypse wear. No humans, NPCs or zombies baked into the background, no bodies or gore, no UI or labels outside the scene, no game HUD, no logos or watermarks, no Fallout franchise symbols, no magical effects, no photorealism, no smooth 3D rendering, no perspective change, no crop, no added rooms. Preserve the full original atlas canvas including both panels.",
